fact WorkItem(id: "WI-751", description: some(value: "Loader/name-resolution: a RULE named after a NAMESPACE ROOT SEGMENT disables every qualified reference under that root in its scope. REPRO (verified): namespace test.x with `rule anthill(?x) :- q(row: ?x)` and `operation useIt() -> List[T = Int64] = anthill.prelude.List.nil()` fails with `anthill.prelude.List.nil.apply: expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor`; the byte-identical control with the rule renamed to `notanthill` loads clean. Any user rule (or, by the same mechanism, any other scope-local symbol) sharing a name with a root namespace segment silently disables the whole import surface under that root -- action at a distance from one unrelated declaration, and the diagnostic names the qualified call rather than the colliding rule, so the cause is not discoverable from the error. ROOT CAUSE (known precisely): remap_name_str_inner (load.rs ~6645) resolves a dotted name by falling through resolve_in_scope -> NotFound -> resolve_dotted_by_head(name, scope) (load.rs ~6708), which splits the name on the FIRST dot and resolves the HEAD SEGMENT IN SCOPE. With `rule anthill` in the enclosing namespace the head `anthill` resolves to that RULE (nearer scope wins over the top-level namespace), so the probe appends the tail to the RULE's qualified path and looks up `test.x.anthill.prelude.List.nil`, which does not exist. The remaining fallbacks do not rescue it: implicit_qualified only covers the kernel/prelude short-name vocab, forbid_if_internal only reclassifies internal-visibility misses, so the name is interned as a bare unknown symbol (WI-476 path) and the typer reports `unknown functor`. NOT A WI-750 REGRESSION -- A/B-confirmed IDENTICAL at 140246e7 (pre-WI-750) and at f3a31617: same fixture, same error. WI-750 is adjacent only in that its first cut ALSO mis-decomposed this shape via the re-route prefix scan (`Relation.prelude: no such member`); the delivered gate stops the mis-decomposition and hands the name back to this resolver, where the pre-existing miss remains. THE ASYMMETRY THAT POINTS AT THE FIX: WI-750's loader-side probe `qualified_name_resolves` (load.rs, dot_call_receiver_chain) checks `by_qualified_name.contains_key(name)` BEFORE head-qualification, for exactly this reason -- an ABSOLUTE name stays a symbol's own qualified name no matter what a same-spelled rule does to the head. The MAIN resolver has no such check, so the re-route probe currently knows something about absolute names that remap_name_str_inner does not. FIX DIRECTION (starting hypothesis, settle precedence deliberately): consult by_qualified_name for the WHOLE dotted name in remap_name_str_inner's NotFound branch, ahead of (or alongside) resolve_dotted_by_head, subject to the same qualified_visible gate the head-qualified hit already passes through. THE DESIGN QUESTION IS PRECEDENCE, not plumbing: does a name that IS some symbol's fully-qualified name outrank a scope-local head that merely shares its first segment? Argument for yes -- the collision is between a WHOLE name and a strict PREFIX of it, and shadowing compares whole names (the same reasoning that settled WI-750's `let app = 1; app.helpers.Helper.twice(2)` regression). Mind the WI-749 footgun in the neighbourhood: a rule LABEL may itself be DOTTED (`rule a.b:` defines the LITERAL key `a.b` in scope locals), so a whole-name lookup and a scope lookup can BOTH hit for the same text and the order decides the answer -- do not add this as an unordered `or_else`, place it in a stated ladder as WI-750 did. CONSIDER ALSO (may be the better answer, or a companion): whether a rule/sort name colliding with a visible namespace root should be a LOUD load-time ambiguity rather than silently winning the head slot -- resolve_in_scope already returns Ambiguous for several neighbouring collision shapes, and that guard is what incidentally shields other cases (noted during the WI-750 review); making this collision explicit would fit the repo principle of a loud error over a silent wrong answer. ACCEPTANCE: the repro above loads and evaluates with `rule anthill` present; a scope-local rule/sort sharing a name with a namespace root no longer suppresses qualified references under that root (or is rejected loudly at load with a diagnostic naming the COLLISION, if that route is chosen instead); the chosen precedence is documented as an ordered ladder, not an incidental `or_else`, and a test pins the dotted-rule-LABEL interaction (rules `a` and `a.b` in scope, both spellings) so the WI-749 ordering footgun cannot reappear; WI-714/WI-723/WI-729/WI-749/WI-750 suites unaffected; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. SCOPE: Rust-only (scaland has no operation loading and no dot-call/name re-route). Found during the WI-750 /code-review pass (2026-07-18), reproduced by two independent reviewers and confirmed pre-existing by an A/B build against HEAD."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T11:59:16Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-751", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION -- THE TICKET'S REPRO DOES NOT REPRODUCE. `rule anthill(?x) :- q(row: ?x)` loads clean at HEAD (402034c5), with or without the prelude import; the \"REPRO (verified)\" and \"A/B-confirmed IDENTICAL at 140246e7 and f3a31617\" claims are both false. A HEAD-PREDICATE rule does not take the name slot: scan_rule_goal (load.rs ~1711) mints the head-functor Goal ONLY when resolve_in_scope(functor_name) is NotFound, so with the namespace visible no Goal is defined and the head keeps resolving to the namespace. The declarations that DO occupy the head slot, measured: a LABELLED rule (`rule myroot: r(?x)`), a `sort`, an `operation`. A `let` binder does not (the head rung reads resolve_in_scope, which is blind to binders). The defect, mechanism and root cause were otherwise exactly as described -- it was never rule-specific, and the headline should read \"a scope-local declaration named after a namespace root\". PRECEDENCE ANSWERED, AGAINST THE TICKET'S HYPOTHESIS. The ticket proposed consulting by_qualified_name \"ahead of (or alongside)\" resolve_dotted_by_head. Ahead is WRONG and was verified so: with the absolute rung first, `x.foo()` inside `outer.user` silently returns the TOP-LEVEL `x.foo` (99) instead of the sibling `outer.x.foo` (41) -- WI-751's own disease in the opposite direction, any top-level namespace capturing paths a nearer same-rooted namespace already answers. Head-qualification is SCOPE-RELATIVE and must outrank a bare global path. Pinned by wi751_scope_relative_root_beats_a_top_level_namespace, which fails if the rungs are swapped. THE TRAP THAT COST A ROUND: \"head-qualification MISSED\" is NOT \"the name resolves to nothing\", so the rung is not purely additive (the first cut shipped that claim in a code comment as its justification). On a PARTIAL miss -- head resolving correctly to a nearer namespace, only a later segment absent -- it silently re-rooted the whole path at a same-spelled TOP-LEVEL namespace, detectable only by a downstream return-type mismatch. Caught by the /code-review pass, reproduced independently, then guarded by head_owns_path: skip the absolute rung when the path's head resolves in scope to a NAMESPACE (it owns every path beneath it, so a missing member is a member miss, not a licence to re-root) or resolves AMBIGUOUS (a reportable finding, not something to resolve past). DELIVERED AS FOUR CHANGES, not the one sketched: (1) absolute_qualified -- dotted-only by_qualified_name rung ranked BELOW head-qualification; (2) head_owns_path -- the partial-miss/ambiguous guard; (3) a SymbolKind::Field refusal in resolve_dotted_by_head -- entity fields register as <entity_qn>.<field>, so `sort data { entity user(name: Int64) }` supplies a complete <ns>.data.user.name and head-qualification HITS, capturing `data.user.name()` from namespace data.user; that is the HIT half of the defect and the absolute rung alone cannot reach it; (4) the same rung in resolve_qualified_rule_readonly -- shipping it only in remap_name_str_inner fixed the applied CALL and left the BARE rule-reference citation (`myroot.inner.rel.isEmpty`, `.takeN(1)`) broken, splitting the WI-729/749/750 citation forms that function exists to keep identical. LOUD-AMBIGUITY ALTERNATIVE EVALUATED AND DECLINED, and the ticket's premise for it is inaccurate: resolve_in_scope_recursive returns a LOCAL immediately (intern.rs ~491) and never consults parents, so Ambiguous arises only AMONG PARENTS -- local-vs-namespace-root is structurally not an ambiguity in that resolver, and local_shadows_parent pins local-wins as deliberate. Making it one is a new check that would reject every `sort data` / `operation app` sharing a spelling with a visible namespace root, breaking broad legitimate shadowing for a collision that only matters when a path under that root is actually written. Ladder documented in docs/kernel-language.md 8.6 (spec was silent on the dotted rungs entirely). STILL OPEN, all verified, deliberately out of scope -- worth their own tickets: (a) remap_name (TYPE position) has the absolute rung but NO head-qualification, so `util.T` fails where `util.f()` resolves -- the opposite ladder one position over, and fixing it adds a rung ABOVE an existing one, which can change what existing type names mean; (b) resolve_name_in_kb_opt (query patterns) ranks absolute FIRST with no dotted gate, so a query and the program it queries can bind the same text to different symbols; (c) qualified_name_resolves ranks absolute FIRST while the resolver ranks it LAST, so a re-route can decline on a hit the resolver would rank below a local reading; (d) a head-qualified hit that fails the internal gate is terminal and never falls through to the absolute rung. Tests: 9 in wi751_namespace_root_shadow_test.rs; full workspace 3013 passed / 0 failed via rustland/scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-18T11:59:05Z")
